WEBVTT

00:00:01.290 --> 00:00:02.326
Hi, I'm Gimlet!

00:00:02.326 --> 00:00:06.126
Today, how the sandbox behind Claude Code's shell boxes a command.

00:00:08.517 --> 00:00:11.325
The whole Linux sandbox is one command line.

00:00:11.325 --> 00:00:12.729
Mounts decide every file.

00:00:12.729 --> 00:00:16.238
The network is empty, except one socket to a proxy.

00:00:19.508 --> 00:00:22.361
First, the whole host disk is mounted read only.

00:00:22.361 --> 00:00:24.262
Reads are open; writes are refused.

00:00:24.682 --> 00:00:27.673
The project folder is bound back on top, writable.

00:00:27.673 --> 00:00:29.002
Writes land only here.

00:00:29.422 --> 00:00:33.041
Then the files that run code later, shell startup files and git

00:00:33.041 --> 00:00:34.549
hooks, are frozen on top.

00:00:34.969 --> 00:00:37.448
A secret folder gets an empty mount over it.

00:00:37.448 --> 00:00:38.825
Its files are not hidden.

00:00:38.825 --> 00:00:39.651
They are gone.

00:00:41.025 --> 00:00:44.629
The network is removed; a helper runs the command one level down.

00:00:45.049 --> 00:00:48.483
A relay starts inside the box, the only path to the proxy.

00:00:48.903 --> 00:00:52.511
It waits until the relay is listening, so the command never races it.

00:00:52.931 --> 00:00:57.439
A helper nests fresh namespaces, so the command cannot see the processes around it.

00:00:57.859 --> 00:01:01.466
A kernel filter blocks local sockets, so the command cannot slip

00:01:01.466 --> 00:01:02.777
past the network fence.

00:01:03.197 --> 00:01:08.289
A second filter refuses unsharing and mounting, so the command cannot undo its denials.

00:01:08.289 --> 00:01:09.380
Then it runs.

00:01:11.175 --> 00:01:14.496
A write outside the project and a denied host both fail; a

00:01:14.496 --> 00:01:16.156
write to the project is allowed.

00:01:16.576 --> 00:01:19.939
Every connection leaves through a socket to the proxy, which allows a

00:01:19.939 --> 00:01:22.180
known host and asks about a new one.

00:01:23.717 --> 00:01:27.790
On my machine, the sandbox added about eighty six milliseconds a command,

00:01:27.790 --> 00:01:29.148
against six without it.

00:01:30.642 --> 00:01:34.002
You can read the whole boundary in one command line: mounts for

00:01:34.002 --> 00:01:35.962
the files, one socket for the network.

00:01:39.067 --> 00:01:40.210
Mounts for the files.

00:01:40.210 --> 00:01:41.639
One socket for the network.

00:01:41.639 --> 00:01:43.353
A filter for the side doors.

00:01:43.773 --> 00:01:45.883
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:45.883 --> 00:01:46.485
I'm Gimlet.

00:01:46.485 --> 00:01:46.787
Bye!
